let __v_4718 = Value::from("Make two loops and tie them like a bow: cross the laces once, take the top one under and through the gap so the base knot is snug, then bend each lace into a loop, cross the two loops over, and push one loop down through the hole beneath them. Pull the loops outward rather than up, and it sits flat. Two things make the rest easy: lace from the bottom up so every row lies flat, and leave about 10cm of lace after the bow - a long tail is what drags and comes undone.");
frame.store("_LACE_KNOT", __v_4718.clone())?;
let __v_4719 = Value::from("About five or six, and it comes quicker if the lace is the practice rather than the shoe: tie a lace round a table leg, a ribbon round a chair back, or one big shoe on the floor, and teach the bunny-ears version first - two loops, cross, push one through. Say one step at a time and let them repeat it five times before adding the next, and be slow in front of them with your own laces. Fifteen minutes on a Saturday works better than twenty rushed tries on the way to school, and being allowed to be bad at it for a few weeks is part of learning it.");
frame.store("_LACE_TEACHING", __v_4719.clone())?;
let __v_4720 = Value::from("It is the knot rather than your tying: a bow on a smooth lace works loose. Tie the first knot twice - a double knot - for round, waxed or nylon laces, or switch to flat laces with a bit of texture, and check you have about 10cm of tail after the bow because a long end swings and pulls itself free. Running for a bus or a bike ride: tuck the ends and loops under the criss-cross above them and they will stay put all day.");
frame.store("_LACE_UNDONE_REPLY", __v_4720.clone())?;
let __v_4721 = Value::from("Skates want a firmer lace than shoes and nothing left trailing: lace from the bottom, keep the foot snug through the middle, then pull the top two tight at the ankle so the heel cannot lift, double knot it, and tuck the ends inside the top of the boot. Do them up with thin socks or warm feet, because tight laces on cold feet is how toes go numb, and loosen the top two before you take them off.");
frame.store("_LACE_SKATES_REPLY", __v_4721.clone())?;
let __v_4722 = Value::from("Use a ribbon rather than string, because it holds its shape: make a loop in each hand, cross the right loop over the left, tuck it behind and bring it up through the gap underneath, then pull both loops outward until the bow is the size you want. Tape the ribbon to the box underneath first if the present is heavy, so it cannot slide, and curl the tails over a scissor blade and cut them on the angle.");
frame.store("_LACE_BOW_REPLY", __v_4722.clone())?;
